Here's a JavaScript function that takes an array of 5 numbers as input and checks if any of the numbers is a valid prime number. If a prime number is found, it will print the number to the console.

```javascript
function checkPrime(arr) {
  for (let I = 0; I < arr.length; i++) {
    const num = arr[i];
    if (num <= 1) continue; // 1 and below are not prime

    for (let j = 2; j <= Math.sqrt(num); j++) {
      if (num % j === 0) {
        // num is divisible by j, so it's not prime
        continue;
      }
    }

    console.log(`Found prime number: ${num}`);
    break; // once a prime is found, we don't need to check further
  }
}

// Example usage
const numbers = [2, 4, 5, 7, 11];
checkPrime(numbers);
```

This function uses a nested loop to iterate through each number in the input array. For each number, it checks if it's greater than 1 (since 1 and below are not prime). Then, it iterates from 2 to the square root of the number to check for factors. If a factor is found, the loop continues to the next number in the array.

If a prime number is found, the function logs the number to the console and breaks out of the loop, since we only need to find one prime number. If no prime number is found, the function will not log anything to the console.

